      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">a microporous polyethylene membrane</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">was produced in the same manner as in Example 5 except for using a polyethylene composition comprising 30% by mass of UHMWPE having Mw of 2.2 ⁇ 10 6 and 70% by mass of HDPE having Mw of 3.0 ⁇ 10 5 , which had T (20%) of 126.1° C., T (60%) of 135.9° C., and  ⁇  0 of 2.1 rad/sec.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0201">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">a microporous PE membrane</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">was produced in the same manner as in Example 5 except for using a polyethylene composition comprising 30% by mass of UHMWPE having Mw of 2.2 ⁇ 10 6 , 40% by mass of HDPE having Mw of 3.0 ⁇ 10 5 , and 30% by mass of LMPE having Mw of 2.0 ⁇ 10 3 , which had T (20%) of 122° C., T (60%) of 133.6° C., and  ⁇  0 of 10.2 rad/sec, and changing the heat-setting temperature to 117° C.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0202">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">a microporous PE membrane</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">was produced in the same manner as in Example 5 except for using a polyethylene composition comprising 30% by mass of UHMWPE having Mw of 2.2 ⁇ 10 6 , 60% by mass of HDPE having Mw of 3.0 ⁇ 10 5 , and 10% by mass of HDPE having Mw of 2.0 ⁇ 10 5 , which had T (20%) of 124.3° C., T (60%) of 135.8° C., and  ⁇  0 of 10.3 rad/sec.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0203">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">microporous polyethylene membranes obtained in Examples 5 to 7 and Comparative Examples 4 to 6</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">were measured with respect to average thickness, air permeability, porosity, pin puncture strength, tensile rupture strength, tensile rupture elongation, heat shrinkage ratio, shutdown temperature, air permeability change ratio and meltdown temperature by the above methods.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0204">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">a curve shown in FIG. 7</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">which showed the relation between the temperature T and a reciprocal of the air permeability p was prepared from the data of the temperature T and the air permeability p obtained by shutdown temperature measurement.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0204">
